      "section": "who",
      "overlays": {
        "lowerThird": {
          "name": "Frederick Douglass",
          "sub": "c. 1818 – 1895"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "born",
      "text": "Born enslaved in Maryland, he escaped in 1838, disguised as a sailor.",
      "visual": {
        "kind": "card",
        "image": "fugitive",
        "crop": [
          0.0,
          0.0,
          1.0,
          0.62
        ],
        "caption": "\"The Fugitive's Song\", dedicated to Douglass, 1845"
      },
      "section": "story",
      "overlays": {
        "pin": {
          "label": "Maryland",
          "sub": "born enslaved"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "speaker",
      "text": "In the North, he became one of the most powerful voices against slavery.",
      "visual": {
        "kind": "photo",
        "image": "met1855",
        "focus": [
          0.48,
          0.4
        ],
        "zoom": [
          1.3,
          1.38
        ],
        "anchor": [
          0.45,
          0.35
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "cartoons",
      "text": "But most pictures of Black Americans in his time were demeaning, made to mock them.",
      "visual": {
        "kind": "title",
        "title": "Mocked.",
        "sub": "in cartoons, prints and posters"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "camera",
      "text": "So Douglass turned to the camera.",
      "visual": {
        "kind": "photo",
        "image": "young1855",
        "focus": [
          0.48,
          0.45
        ],
        "zoom": [
          1.0,
          1.06
        ],
        "anchor": [
          0.45,
          0.4
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "sat",
      "text": "From 1841 until his death in 1895, he sat for his portrait whenever he could.",
      "visual": {
        "kind": "title",
        "title": "1841 – 1895",
        "sub": "whenever he could"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "count",
      "text": "More than 160 times.",
      "visual": {
        "kind": "photo",
        "image": "p1860",
        "focus": [
          0.55,
          0.5
        ],
        "zoom": [
          1.05,
          1.12
        ],
        "anchor": [
          0.46,
          0.4
        ]
      },
      "overlays": {
        "stamp": "160+"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "dignity",
      "text": "Each photo showed what slavery had tried to deny him: his dignity.",
      "visual": {
        "kind": "photo",
        "image": "met1855",
        "focus": [
          0.48,
          0.35
        ],
        "zoom": [
          1.6,
          1.7
        ],
        "anchor": [
          0.45,
          0.3
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "lectures",
      "text": "He even wrote lectures on the power of pictures.",
      "visual": {
        "kind": "title",
        "title": "Pictures",
        "sub": "he lectured on their power"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "twist",
      "text": "By the end of his life, he was the most photographed American of the 1800s. More than Abraham Lincoln.",
      "visual": {
        "kind": "photo",
        "image": "p1879",
        "focus": [
          0.5,
          0.42
        ],
        "zoom": [
          1.1,
          1.18
        ],
        "anchor": [
          0.45,
          0.36
        ]
      },
      "section": "cost"
    },
    {
      "id": "legacy",
      "text": "They tried to turn Black Americans into cartoons. He answered with his own face.",
      "visual": {
        "kind": "photo",
        "image": "miller",
        "focus": [
          0.53,
          0.33
        ],
        "zoom": [
          1.05,
          1.12
        ],
        "anchor": [
          0.45,
          0.3
